The `announce` politeness level only checks `errorCount > 0`, but the PR description explicitly states it should be `assertive` when component test errors **or** accessibility errors are present. `a11yErrorCount` is scoped inside the `if (hasA11yAddon)` block above and is inaccessible at the call site, so when there are a11y errors but no component errors the announcement is incorrectly `polite` instead of `assertive`.

Fix: hoist `a11yErrorCount` out of the `if (hasA11yAddon)` block and include it in the level condition:
```tsx
let a11yErrorCount = 0;
if (hasA11yAddon) {
  a11yErrorCount = a11yStatusValueToStoryIds['status-value:error'].length;
  // ... push to parts as before
}
announce(message, errorCount > 0 || a11yErrorCount > 0 ? 'assertive' : 'polite');
```
Note: the new story `AnnouncesTestRunFinished` doesn't cover this case (it always has a component error), so this bug goes untested.

No TypeScript module augmentation is declared for this custom Vitest matcher. Without it, calls like `expect(container).toHaveLiveRegion(...)` will produce a TypeScript type error (`Property 'toHaveLiveRegion' does not exist on type 'Assertion'`). The standard fix is to add a declaration either in this file or in a companion `.d.ts`:
```ts
declare module 'vitest' {
  interface Assertion<R = any> {
    toHaveLiveRegion(options: LiveRegionMatcherOptions): R;
  }
  interface AsymmetricMatchersContaining {
    toHaveLiveRegion(options: LiveRegionMatcherOptions): void;
  }
}
```
This is needed in both `toHaveLiveRegion.ts` (for test-file consumers) and wherever `expect.extend` is called in story files.

There is a test that `role="log"` matches by text, but unlike `role="status"` (line 55) and `role="alert"` (line 62), there is no test asserting that `role="log"` correctly infers `polite` when filtering by level. The implementation at `toHaveLiveRegion.ts:44` treats `log` as polite — add a matching test case to prevent regressions:
```ts
it('infers polite level from role="log"', () => {
  const container = document.createElement('div');
  container.innerHTML = '<div role="log">Log entry</div>';
  expect(container).toHaveLiveRegion({ text: 'Log entry', level: 'polite' });
  expect(() => {
    expect(container).toHaveLiveRegion({ text: 'Log entry', level: 'assertive' });
  }).toThrow();
});
```
